owns but no explicit route matches. `deny` — the value the Job Manager schema ships with — turns the implicit denial of unmapped paths into an explicit, auditable statement; `allow` opens unmapped endpoints under that prefix to any identity that matches an active principal."],[2016,"**`EXPIRED`**: it has passed its expiration date. The monitor detects this periodically; it is not a state that should be assigned manually."],[2714,"**`helm upgrade --wait` times out and `helm status` reports the release as `failed`.** With local KMS or VA storage this is expected on the first run: the new pods cannot become ready before their data is copied. The hooks ran and the manifests and PVCs were applied, so complete step 4 and re-run the same `helm upgrade` command. If other pods are also not ready, check their logs, because something else is wrong."],[2716,"**`helm upgrade` fails with an unknown-key validation error.** Remove or rename the legacy values — typically `toolbox`, `auth.oidc.apiGateway` or `auth.authorization` — because the strict schema rejects keys it does not know."],[2018,"**`INACTIVE`**: it is disabled without representing a definitive revocation."],[2017,"**`REVOKED`**: it was invalidated before expiring, with a reason and a timestamp."],[503,"**`skip_authz: true` on a route** allows any identity that matches an active principal to reach it, even one with no granted policies. Use it for paths such as `/openapi.json` or `/health` that every authenticated caller should be able to access. The request still has to authenticate successfully: a credential that fails validation or matches no active principal is rejected before this setting is considered. A `skip_authz` route cannot declare constraints, and it wins over any other schema's route or default action for the same request."],[1477,"**`UPDATE_CERTIFICATE`**: written when a certificate's preventive renewal window activates — that is, when the `triggered` field of the preventive renewal delta flips from `false` to `true`. This action tells the device it must start the re-enrollment process to renew its certificate before it expires."],[1478,"**`UPDATE_TRUST_ANCHOR_LIST`**: written to all devices of a DMS when the DMS's `ManagedCAs` list changes. This action tells the device it must update its trusted CA store."],[779,"**Active**"],[2681,"**Add `services.authz`.** Configure the JWKS endpoint, the database mappings and the bootstrap principal:"],[1703,"**Administration**"],[2221,"**Algorithm, Key Size & Strength**: algorithm, parameters and strength level."],[2236,"**Algorithm**: must match the algorithm used for the signature."],[2229,"**Algorithm**: signing algorithm available for the key type."],[2186,"**Aliases**: alternative names associated with the key."],[2219,"**Aliases**: alternative names."],[2728,"**AMQP.** The backend connects to the broker with the expected TLS settings, and the cleanup policy for SSE queues is in place."],[2734,"**API clients.** Scripts and consoles no longer use the old DMS layout (flat `additional_validation_cas`, or the legacy `mtls`, `apikey` and `oidc` fields), send DMS updates with the ID in the URL, and match on the new certificate `type` values."],[663,"**API-key headers are restricted.** A custom `apikey_options.header` may not use names that the platform or receiving services trust — for example `X-Principal-Id`, `X-Request-Id`, `Host`, `Content-Type` or `Forwarded` — and no name may start with `X-Forwarded-`, `X-Envoy-` or `X-Lms-`. Comparisons are case-insensitive."],[521,"**Atomic actions** are evaluated on a concrete instance. For certificates these include `read`, `metadata-update`, `status-update` and `delete`; for a CA there are also `sign`, `reissue` and other lifecycle operations."],[586,"**Audit**: reviews configuration and activity without creating, modifying, signing, revoking or deleting."],[668,"**Authorities:** `ca.create`, `ca.import`, `ca.reissue` and `ca.delete`."],[583,"**Authorization administration**: manages principals, policies and grants. It should belong to a small, separate group."],[2727,"**Authorization.** Sign in as the bootstrap principal and confirm administration works, check that an identity without permissions receives `403`, and review client and integration permissions."],[587,"**Automation**: uses a technical account or a separate certificate, with permissions limited to the flow and resources it controls."],[1530,"**CA and profile:** prepare the issuer and certificate rules in PKI."],[2361,"**CA Certificate Expiration**: for example, one year from creation."],[2359,"**CA Name**: `Acme Evaluation Root CA`."],[652,"**Call Timeout** defaults to 10 seconds. The console rejects a value above 30 seconds with `Webhook timeout must be at most 30s.`; the API clamps the same value to the cap instead (see Calls are bounded)."],[661,"**Calls are bounded.** A call defaults to a 10 second timeout; a configured `call_timeout` is capped at 30 seconds. Values above the cap are clamped, not rejected."],[1502,"**Cause:** Every full DMS edit (`PUT /v1/dms/{id}`) triggers a `CreateProvisioningTemplate` attempt for the template named after the DMS ID, and the call is not idempotent: when the template already exists, it fails, the event is retried three times and finally lands in `lamassu-dlq`."],[1487,"**Cause:** Most commonly the certificate was provisioned in JITP mode. JITP certificates lack the connector's metadata key and are outside the state synchronization mechanism. If the DMS is in `auto` mode, it may indicate the initial provisioning did not complete correctly and the AWS ARN was not stored in the certificate metadata."],[1484,"**Cause:** The connector is not running or is not subscribed to the event bus. It can also indicate incorrect IAM credentials, insufficient permissions or that the metadata key was written with a wrong `connector_id`."],[1490,"**Cause:** The device uses an MQTT client ID different from the certificate's CN. The forced disconnect opens a connection using the CN as client ID; if the firmware uses another identifier, the disconnect does not affect that session."],[1493,"**Cause:** The MQTT client ID does not match the ThingName, which is always the CN of the certificate issued by Lamassu."],[1499,"**Cause:** The shadow feature is disabled in the DMS configuration, the connector's credentials lack IoT data-plane permissions, or the firmware reads a different shadow than the configured one."],[1496,"**Cause:** This is expected behavior while AWS executes the JITP template on the device's first connection."],[1085,"**Certificate and webhook:** requires both controls in the configured operation."],[793,"**Certificate History** relates previous and current versions. Replacing the slot does not by itself revoke every previous version."],[669,"**Certificates:** `ca.sign.certificate` and `certificate.delete`."],[749,"**Certification Authority (CA).** Signs certificates and establishes the chain of trust."],[2689,"**Check AMQP.** If you set `amqp.tls: true`, also set `amqp.port` to the broker's TLS listener (commonly `5671`), or set `amqp.tls: false` to stay on plaintext."],[1083,"**Client certificate:** useful when each device has an initial credential whose key it controls."],[2489,"**Client:** check cache, policy for expired or unavailable evidence, critical IDP extension and rejection of a new connection."],[1532,"**Client:** prepare the initial credential, server trust, and key generation or custody."],[2247,"**Common Name (CN)**: main name of the identity."],[14,"**Compute:** a `t3.large` instance at minimum. Use `t3.xlarge` or higher when the load requires it."],[2726,"**Connectivity.** `helm test \"$RELEASE\" -n $NAMESPACE --logs` passes; the hook checks the CA, DMS Manager, Device Manager and VA health endpoints and the UI response."],[1534,"**Continuity:** configure renewal, successor installation and recovery according to DMS policy."],[2688,"**Convert `services.connectors` to a map** keyed by connector ID, each entry with its `type` and `image`:"],[2702,"**Copy the data, align ownership and verify the copy.** The second command must print `copy verified`; any `diff` output means the copy is incomplete, so do not continue:"],[2250,"**Country (C)**: two-letter country code."],[2488,"**CRL:** compare `latest_crl`, number and timestamps with the downloaded list. Check the serial in its contents. If unchanged, review `regenerate_on_revoke`, event delivery, jobs, signing and storage with the deployment operator."],[2185,"**Crypto Engine**: cryptographic engine keeping the key."],[2208,"**Crypto Engine**: engine that will keep the imported key."],[2198,"**Crypto Engine**: engine that will keep the key."],[2220,"**Crypto Engine**: engine where the key resides."],[748,"**Cryptographic engine.** Generates or safeguards keys and executes private-key operations without exposing key material to the rest of the services."],[2721,"**Databases.** Each service reads the `pki` database with its own schema, `authz` and `wfx` data are reachable, and the migration status is registered per schema, including the CA unique-ID and KMS composite-key migrations."],[789,"**Decommissioned**"],[2362,"**Default End-Entity Certificate Issuance Expiration**: for example, 90 days, within the CA's lifetime."],[2193,"**Delete Key** to remove the key from the system."],[2685,"**Delete the gateway token validation blocks.** Remove `auth.oidc.apiGateway` and `auth.authorization` once their settings are in `services.authz`:"],[960,"**Delete** removes the DMS record and configuration; it does not itself cascade revocation across every device and certificate. Migrate dependencies and retain audit references before retirement."],[794,"**Device Event Timeline** helps reconstruct registration, provisioning, renewal and status changes. A change may be saved even if event persistence fails; also inspect the record and certificates."],[2729,"**Device events.** `GET /v1/devices/:id/events` returns the historical events of a device that had them."],[1649,"**Device ID**: `device-001`."],[750,"**Device Management Service (DMS).** Defines how the devices of a fleet are registered, authenticated and renewed."],[585,"**Device registration**: operates devices and DMSs without receiving permissions over keys or authorization configuration."],[751,"**Device.** Keeps its private key and presents the certificate to prove its identity."],[1519,"**Device**"],[672,"**Devices:** `device.create`, `device.identity.update` and `device.status.update`."],[2063,"**Distributed CAs:** form the content the device obtains through EST `/cacerts`. You can include the service TLS certificate, the enrollment CA and an explicit list of managed CAs. Including the issuer does not automatically add all its ancestors."],[1531,"**DMS policy:** define how the device is admitted, how requests authenticate and which trust is distributed."],[1523,"**DMS**"],[958,"**Edit** changes name, settings and metadata. Updates go through the DMS API. `PUT /v1/dms/{id}` always targets the DMS identified by the URL path: the body carries no `id` field, and any `id` or `creation_ts` in it is silently ignored — authorization is evaluated against the same URL identifier. The body must include a non-empty `name` and a `settings` object; missing either returns HTTP 400 and leaves the stored DMS unchanged. `name` and `settings` are always full replacements, so read the DMS before sending API edits to avoid losing fields. `metadata` is optional: omitting it or sending `null` keeps the stored metadata, a supplied object replaces it entirely, and `{}` clears it (a later GET returns an empty object, not `null`). Old-style bodies that carry the full DMS object keep working while they include `name` and `settings`; unknown fields are ignored. Updates reject an empty `name` with HTTP 400: a DMS whose stored name was zeroed by an earlier update must be renamed before any client round-trips it — the AWS IoT connector’s JITP writeback included."],[2253,"**Email Address**: contact email."],[633,"**Email** sends alerts to a person or an operational list. It only requires the destination address. Event content is rendered as HTML-escaped text, never as markup."],[2690,"**Enable the CA-to-KMS migration** if your CA keys predate the KMS:"],[2486,"**Endpoint:** inspect AIA and CDP. Query those URLs from the consumer’s network; an old route or incorrect issuer can explain a discrepancy."],[2061,"**Enrollment CA:** signs the new identities."],[671,"**Enrollment:** `dms.create`, `dms.update`, `dms.enroll` and `dms.reenroll`."],[2724,"**Existing CRLs are served.** `GET /crl/<ca-ski>` on VA returns a previously issued CRL, and new CRLs are issued."],[2723,"**Existing KMS keys work.** Issue a certificate from a CA that existed before the upgrade. None of the other checks reads a KMS key, so this is the only one that proves the key material was copied."],[785,"**Expired**"],[783,"**Expiring Soon**"],[2696,"**Find the new StatefulSets and PVCs.** Their names depend on the release name and any `nameOverride` or `fullnameOverride`; the PVC name is `<volumeClaimTemplate>-<StatefulSet name>-0`:"],[1525,"**Fleet**"],[2191,"**Generate CSR** to create a PKCS#10 request with the selected key."],[1849,"**Generate Key & CSR in Browser** is useful for a manual operation or a quick test. The browser generates the key and you must download it at the end."],[520,"**Global actions** do not need a resource identifier. `create`, `import` or access to a console section are common examples."],[959,"**Go to DMS owned devices** restricts inventory by owner. **Show/Edit Metadata** contains operational and integration data; consult the integration guide before changing it. &#x2A;*EST (RFC-7030)** helps locate URLs."],[654,"**HTTP Authentication** selects `No Authentication` (the default), `API Key` (API key value and Header Name, `X-API-Key` by default), `OIDC Client Credentials` (Client ID, Client Secret and Well-Known URL) or `Mutual